# Aarogya Core Rules

Read PROJECT.md before making major product or architectural decisions.

Read DATASETS.md before making dataset, training, or evaluation decisions.

## Product Identity

Aarogya is a professional Personal Health and Disaster Companion.

The product has three primary modes:

1. Normal Mode
2. Disaster Mode
3. Survival Mode

The product must preserve this three mode architecture.

The primary demonstration scenario is a sudden Nepal style flash flood.

The system should remain general enough to support:
• Floods
• Fires
• Heat waves
• Cyclones
• Pollution events
• Other supported emergencies

Do not turn Aarogya into a generic fitness application.

Do not turn Aarogya into a disease diagnosis application.

Do not add unrelated AI features merely because they are technically interesting.

## Product Lifecycle

NORMAL
→ personalized health baseline

DISASTER
→ local disaster warning and safety guidance

SURVIVAL
→ stranded user monitoring and rescue assistance

Each mode must have a clearly defined purpose.

## Core USPs

Aarogya's three primary USPs are:

1. Personalized Health Intelligence
2. Decentralized Disaster Intelligence
3. Survivor Health and Rescue Intelligence

New features should strengthen at least one of these.

## Offline First

Critical functionality must work without internet connectivity.

Never make the following dependent on a cloud round trip:

• Risk inference
• Fall detection
• Local health monitoring
• Emergency event creation
• Emergency event storage
• Previously downloaded disaster guidance
• Previously downloaded maps
• Local device communication

Cloud services may provide optional synchronization, remote dashboards, analytics, or message relay.

## Privacy

Health information should remain on the device by default.

Transmit only information that is necessary for a clearly defined purpose.

Never log sensitive health data unnecessarily.

Never commit:
• API keys
• Passwords
• Tokens
• Private certificates
• Personal health records
• Real user location history

## Medical Positioning

Aarogya is a risk detection and emergency assistance system.

It is not a medical diagnostic system.

Use:

"Elevated health risk detected."

"Your readings differ significantly from your usual baseline."

"Possible fall detected."

"Emergency event recorded."

Do not use unsupported statements such as:

"You have a heart attack."

"You are definitely dehydrated."

"You have a medical condition."

"This system will save your life."

Never invent clinical accuracy claims.

## Engineering Quality

Do not create fake functionality simply to make a screen appear complete.

A demo simulation is acceptable when it passes through the real application pipeline.

Correct:

Sensor simulator
→ preprocessing
→ inference
→ risk engine
→ state machine
→ UI

Incorrect:

Button
→ manually change UI
→ pretend AI detected event

Keep simulated inputs isolated from production sensor implementations.

## Dependencies

Do not add a dependency without a concrete reason.

Before adding a package, determine:

• What problem it solves
• Whether the existing stack can solve the problem
• Whether it works offline where required
• Whether it supports Android
• Whether it supports the intended deployment architecture
• Whether it introduces unnecessary complexity

## Existing Code

Before changing an existing feature:

1. Inspect the existing implementation.
2. Understand its dependencies.
3. Identify the smallest required change.
4. Preserve working behavior.
5. Test after modification.

Do not rewrite large sections of the application unnecessarily.

## Product Scope

If a proposed feature does not support:

• Normal health monitoring
• Disaster response
• Survivor assistance
• Privacy
• Offline operation
• Emergency communication

question whether it belongs in Aarogya.

The primary goal is a convincing, technically credible SIH prototype.
